# Binaries
/bin/
/dist/
# ...the built binary at the ROOT, and only there. Without the leading slash this
# pattern matches at any depth, so it also ignored the directory cmd/openledger/ --
# and `cmd/` was never committed at all. `make build` and `make test` both failed on
# a fresh clone, and ADR-0012's central framing ("10,053 lines of SQL against eleven
# lines of Go") rested on a file no reader could see.
/openledger
# ...including the spike binaries. spike003 was listed and spike005 was not, so a
# 16 MB compiled ELF sat in the tree -- in a directory whose own README says the
# code there is throwaway and is not built by CI.
spikes/*/spike0*

# Go
*.test
*.out
coverage.*
vendor/

# Generated data-access code. The question this NOTE used to hold open is settled:
# ADR-0002 is accepted and spike 002 is closed, and the answer is that generated code
# belongs IN version control, because that is what makes a review diff honest. This
# line survives only until a generator and a `make` target for it exist — there is no
# such target today, so do not go looking for one.
/internal/pg/gen/

# Local env / secrets
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Editors
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp

# OS
.DS_Store

# Local postgres volume
/.pgdata/

# Scratch
/tmp/

# Spike codegen output — regenerate with `sqlc generate` in the spike dir
/spikes/*/gen/
spike003
